I’ll first inventory the measurement, chemistry, reusable refinement utilities, and any candidate databases. Then I’ll build a physically constrained baseline, test plausible phase records and polymorphs against residuals, and only retain phases with reproducible diffraction support.

The chemistry points first to zinc tungstate, but at 600 °C unreacted ZnO/WO₃ and WO₃ polymorphs remain credible. I’m treating those as an initial inventory—not assuming a single product—and will use unique peak families plus refined scale stability to decide what survives.

The peak inventory is now coherent: wolframite ZnWO₄ accounts for the broad 18.9°, 30.4/30.7°, 38.3°, 51.7°, and 64–65° families; monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the distinctive 23.17/23.62/24.40° triplet and its 26.6°, 28.6/29.0°, and 33–34° families; wurtzite ZnO is independently marked by 31.79°, 34.46°, 47.57°, 56.60°, 62.87°, and 69.09°. I’ll now refine this three-phase inventory and challenge the WO₃ record/setting under the same profile model.

The neutral common-width model was inadequate: it forced a single broad instrumental width and biased the fractions. Releasing isotropic crystallite size one phase at a time produced stable, non-bound values (about 64 nm ZnWO₄, 122 nm WO₃, 289 nm ZnO), returned the common Gaussian width close to its starting instrumental value, and reduced Rwp from 5.21% to 4.04%. I’m now testing the alternate physically equivalent/nearby WO₃ records because the 23–25° residual is dominated by WO₃ intensity details, not missing peaks.

Both alternate WO₃ refinements are still running through the same staged sequence and full-range replay. The comparison is controlled: identical background order, scales, zero correction, cell flags, common W profile, and phase-size terms; only the WO₃ structural record changes.

A coherent angle-dependent mismatch was shared across all three phases, and refining the previously unknown effective polarization corrected it decisively: Rwp fell from 3.874% to 3.203% with a physical interior value of 0.837. In contrast, U refined negative and is rejected; X gave no material improvement; phase-strain substitutions were worse than size broadening, and added ZnWO₄/ZnO strains became negative, so those are rejected. I’ll re-evaluate asymmetry and the modest WO₃ broadening terms under the accepted polarization baseline.

The full-profile re-challenge of triclinic WO₃ is taking longer because its lower symmetry adds cell variables and stronger parameter coupling. The monoclinic candidate is already converged with GOF 1.121 and no bound profile terms; this remaining comparison will determine whether the slight triclinic advantage seen in the simpler baseline survives the accepted polarization/strain/asymmetry model.

The full-profile comparison now favors the ambient monoclinic WO₃ model (Rwp 3.097%) over triclinic WO₃ (3.125%), while also using fewer cell degrees of freedom; the monoclinic neutron structure has sensible 1.71–2.16 Å W–O distances. Residual peaks are all at modeled families, so no additional crystalline phase is supported. I’m performing the exact full-range save/reopen replay and exporting every submitted artifact from that on-disk project.

Completed the three-phase refinement.

